Problem condition intuition: given two towers x, y, there must be a tower
between them that is tall enough: at least max(xy)+D

Subtask 1: k itself cannot be chosen (unless ans = 1).
We cannot have two x, y both to the left or right of k, as then x or y is the maximum over [x, y], no intermediate tower can possibly be found. This caps the answer at 2, the answer is at least 1 (choose anyone).

We can easily find the peak k, then for each query (l, r), a necessary condition is l < k < r. Furthermore, l is the smallest on left, r is the smallest on the right, and this is the pair most likely to work, so simply check if H[k] >= max(H[l], H[r])+D

Subtask 2: 

Property A: if x can connect y, and y can connect z (x < y < z), then x can connect z. This is very easy to prove.

Then, to check of a clique X works, we can just check all its adjacent elements. For a given query (L,R), let dp[x] = the maximum number of elements chosen among [L, x], with x chosen. We enumerate the last y and obtain a solution O(n^2) with an ST table

Property A will be used implicitly henceforth... also consider this in the proof of Method 1

-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Subtask 3: 

Method 1: greedily choose the towers from shortest to tallest: that is, process ascendingly by height, take a tower whenever it can communicate with the closest left and right towers already chosen (if only left or right then consider only that)! Always choose the shortest. 

Define LD(x) = closest on the left of x such that H[that] >= H[x]+D, similarly RD(x). For x to be successfully added, there cannot be any smaller elements within (LD(x), RD(x)). 

LD, RD can be obtained using a monotonic stack + binary search, or with a segment tree. The positions chosen can be maintained with a fenwick.

Proof of method: suppose x could communicate with closest left and closest right but was not chosen, suppose there is a strictly better choice S with all the smaller elements but without x. 

If A, B with greater heights are to the left and right of x and chosen in S. Then A and B can communicate with intermediary I, and it lies in (A, X) or (X, B). WLOG it lies in (A, X), then we can replace B with X and everything else remains valid. 

If there is only one side of A or B, we can immediately replace whichever it is with X!! everything else still remains valid.

If there is no A or B, then the whole assumption backfires: literally just choose X!!

Therefore, choosing X is always non sub-optimal.

Subtask 4: D = 1, LD(x) is the first to the left smaller than it, similar for RD(x)

Once subtask 3 is obtained, this one is not hard to see...

There are not many possible relations between three adjacent elements:

X > Y < Z: Y is a valley, LD(y) and RD(y) are the positions of X and Z, then, nothing could obstruct Y from being chosen. Y is chosen.

X < Y < Z or X > Y > Z. Consider X < Y < Z:

z is the RD(y), going left from X to LD(y), easily, there must be an element <= X, that has prev >= it. That element is chosen before Y and obstructs its being chosen.

X < Y > Z: the same argument can be made as above, and even better, on both sides :)

Prefix sums O(Q).

