Subtask 5: L = 0, R = N-1

Define LS[x] = closest on the left that is shorter than x, RS similarly.

Lemma 1. x can be chosen whenever there exists a tower between (LS[x], x) such that H[tower] >= H[x]+D and there exists a tower between (x, RS[x]) such that H[tower] >= H[x]+D (*). In other words, let h1 = max of those (LS[x], x) and h2 = max of those (x, RS[x]), then x can be chosen if and only if h1 >= H[x]+d and h2 >= H[x]+D, or D <= min(h1, h2)-H[x]  

Proof. Suppose we have (*), when processing x, focusing on the left: let l be the closest on the left already chosen (like LS[x], it is shorter than x), then (l, x) can definitely be connected, as l <= LS[x]. Same for (r, x), if there is. Furthermore, the greedy shows this is optimal.

Suppose we dont have (*), again focusing on the left, let l be the closest on the left already chosen, we claim that x cannot have been chosen by the greedy. Suppose x was chosen by the greedy, that is, it satisfies the greedy requirement.

Case 1: l < LS[x], specifically, the intermediary I must lie within (l, LS[x]), then LS[x] would have satisfied the greedy requirement, contradicting that l was closest. 

Case 2: l = LS[x], this contradicts the restriction that l < LD[x], and x cannot have been chosen. 

Thus, we precompute h1, h2 for all L, R, and obtain a list of p[x] = min(h1, h2)-H[x], sort and lower_bound.

O(Q log N)


Subtask 6: D is uniform

Run a greedily over all towers. Suppose a1 < a2 < ... < ak were chosen.

Suppose our interval [l,r] intercepts as follows

a{i} < l <= a{i+1} < ... < a{j-1} <= r < a{j}

Inductively consider x by increasing height:

We show that any x with a{k} < x < a{k+1} (i+1 <= k < j-1) cannot be chosen given this interval. When x was considered globally, it was rejected because there existed a smaller chosen one in (LD[x], RD[x]), this means one of a{k}, a{k+1} lies in this interval. 

We show that any x at a{k} remains chosen. This is clear because no smaller element can suddenly appear in (LD[x], RD[x])


